description:
src/sieve.rs:19 declares `const MAX_SIEVE_SCRIPTS: usize = 100;` as a private constant, and `handle_sieve_set` (src/sieve.rs:440) enforces it on every backend without any opt-out or override path:

```
if all_scripts_before_create.len() + successful_creates >= MAX_SIEVE_SCRIPTS {
    not_created.insert(creation_id, set_error_value(&SetError::new(SetErrorType::OverQuota)));
    continue;
}
```

There is no `SieveBackend` method to advertise the per-account script count cap. The doc comment on the constant says "A real backend would derive this from the account's `SieveScripts` capability, but for the test backend a hard ceiling of 100 is a reasonable default." — but this hard ceiling is enforced on EVERY backend, not just the test backend. Third-party backends (kith, stoa, future production deployments) that want a higher or lower cap, or per-account caps, are silently clamped at 100 by handler-internal logic they cannot see.

Per workspace AGENTS.md "Backend caps and limits": "Workspace policy is that caps are NOT spec-mandated. New extensions SHOULD NOT add cap-advertising fields to their JMAP capability objects." But the policy also requires that caps be **backend-configurable**, either via per-method getters (the older mail-server style, e.g. `max_body_value_bytes(caller, account_id)`) or via a `*Limits` struct (the newer chat-server style). MAX_SIEVE_SCRIPTS conforms to neither.

Library-kit posture (workspace AGENTS.md "What this workspace builds"): consumers bring quota/limits machinery; they cannot work around a handler-internal hardcoded cap unless they fork the crate or patch the handler. This is a contract bug — the cap value belongs on `SieveBackend` as a method (sibling to `max_sieve_script_bytes`).

Cookie-cutter amplification: if this exists here (the canonical extension-server template), it almost certainly exists in sibling `*-server` crates that copy the sieve.rs pattern (chat, calendars, tasks, etc.) for analogous per-account caps. Suggested cross-check during fix.

Fix: add `fn max_sieve_scripts_per_account(&self, caller: &Self::CallerCtx, account_id: &jmap_types::Id) -> usize { 100 }` to `SieveBackend` with default `100` to preserve current behavior, replace `MAX_SIEVE_SCRIPTS` constant reference in handler with a call to the trait method.

Priority P0 because: (1) silently locks consumers into a value they cannot change, (2) the workspace's documented library-kit posture mandates backend configurability of all caps, (3) the failure mode is "overQuota at 100 even when backend says capacity is 10000" — silent rejection of valid client requests with no signal of where the constraint comes from.
